<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Newar people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Newars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Newars are the historic inhabitants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, speaking Nepal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhasa">Bhasa</a> (Newari), a Sino-Tibetan language, and following both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist traditions. Under the Malla kings their cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, Patan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a> flourished as centres of trade, temple architecture, metalwork and painting. Newar merchants long dominated trade between Nepal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/newar-people.jpg" alt="Newar people" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krish Dulal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bel_Marriage02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
